  "query_preview": "..\n(Tomorrow through Thursday)\nIssued at 111 PM CDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nThe aforementioned shortwave trough will strengthen as it heads\nsoutheast over the Plains Friday into Saturday, as the 500mb ridge\ncenter shifts west to the Desert Southwest. The band of elevated \nshowers and storms during the morning ahead of the system will \ngradually fade as it heads southeast across the region. By \nafternoon, the surface cool front will be dropping south into NE \nOK and far NW AR and will continue south across the remainder of \nthe forecast area Friday night. Ahead of the front, another day of\nheadline level heat is expected, while some uncertainties due to \ncloud cover and frontal timing remain, especially across the \nnorth. Models show potential for isolated to scattered storms \nalong the front late in the day and into the evening. Deep layer \nshear is not particularly strong over our area, but improves \ncloser to the surface and upper low track over MO. Thus, severe \npotential looks pretty low. \n\nRelatively cooler and drier air will filter into the region \nbehind the front, yielding a multi-day break from the oppressive\nheat for the weekend into Monday.\n\nRidging aloft will eventually build back over the southern CONUS \nlater in the week, and southerly winds will return along with\ngradually increasing humidity. Highs are expected to approach\ntriple digits by the latter part of next week in some areas. The\nforecast looks dry for most areas, with possibly some low chances\nreturning for mid to late week in the terrain of SE OK and NW AR.\n\nLacy",
